[SPEAKER_14]: It seems redundant, however, I trust SMMA is performing the sort of real-time bookkeeping.

549
00:50:01,680 --> 00:50:10,207
[SPEAKER_14]: So as we look at these and make decisions, they get crossed off and we can officially cross them off, I guess, in an October meeting.

550
00:50:11,188 --> 00:50:22,458
[SPEAKER_14]: But I just want to make sure that we don't lose track of all of the proposals, because again, this is a very large building.

551
00:50:22,698 --> 00:50:22,918
[SPEAKER_14]: Thank you.

552
00:50:24,574 --> 00:50:24,654
[SPEAKER_20]: you.

553
00:50:24,674 --> 00:50:25,074
[SPEAKER_20]: So Miller.

554
00:50:25,095 --> 00:50:27,317
[SPEAKER_20]: Hi.

555
00:50:28,157 --> 00:50:29,739
[SPEAKER_20]: Um so I had a question.

556
00:50:29,799 --> 00:50:36,866
[SPEAKER_20]: I know that the current building has an external entrance directly into the studio for Medford Community Media.

557
00:50:37,787 --> 00:50:41,610
[SPEAKER_20]: This current design that's being shown does not have that direct entrance.

558
00:50:41,670 --> 00:50:41,791
[SPEAKER_20]: It's

559
00:50:47,084 --> 00:51:09,231
[SPEAKER_20]: you know section i guess it does not have the direct entry and i was wondering if that is intentional what the thinking behind that is is that irrelevant at this point and we are only thinking about the space but it is something different than i than we currently have and i just wanted to check on that matt rice can you take that question

560
00:51:09,819 --> 00:51:11,100
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, happy to take it.

561
00:51:11,440 --> 00:51:15,844
[SPEAKER_05]: And I am going to say, since you used the word, it's irrelevant in terms of the discussion this evening.

562
00:51:15,884 --> 00:51:18,406
[SPEAKER_05]: And I'm not meaning to indicate, Lisa, it's an irrelevant question.

563
00:51:18,446 --> 00:51:21,289
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a good question so that everyone can understand.

564
00:51:21,409 --> 00:51:29,776
[SPEAKER_05]: But because these plans are very much in a state of flux, it is something that may remain the same as it is shown here.

565
00:51:29,916 --> 00:51:34,680
[SPEAKER_05]: It may result in the offices for Medford Community Media and or the studio.

566
00:51:35,140 --> 00:51:36,781
[SPEAKER_05]: getting closer to the exterior wall.

567
00:51:37,442 --> 00:51:42,005
[SPEAKER_05]: There's a lot of considerations when it comes to that direct access.

568
00:51:42,025 --> 00:51:44,927
[SPEAKER_05]: Certainly Kevin's staff and his input is critical to that.

569
00:51:45,568 --> 00:51:51,572
[SPEAKER_05]: But there's also larger building staffing and security issues associated with where that community entrance is.

570
00:51:52,392 --> 00:51:54,374
[SPEAKER_05]: So it may be exactly the same as it is today.

571
00:51:54,414 --> 00:51:55,775
[SPEAKER_05]: At the end of the day it may be different.

572
00:51:56,495 --> 00:52:04,980
[SPEAKER_05]: I can't say right now whether one of those has a positive or a negative connotation associated with it, but something that we're currently in the process of developing as we go.

573
00:52:05,000 --> 00:52:14,685
[SPEAKER_05]: So I think everyone just will have to give us a little bit of grace and patience as we sort through all the different conversations for the impacted individuals and programs.

574
00:52:16,947 --> 00:52:17,287
[SPEAKER_20]: Thank you.

575
00:52:17,307 --> 00:52:19,508
[SPEAKER_20]: All right.

576
00:52:21,920 --> 00:52:22,520
[SPEAKER_19]: Thank you so much.

577
00:52:22,881 --> 00:52:37,149
[SPEAKER_19]: It's just a quick question I know, based off the recommendation that the current structure of the educational like the, like the plan of the day scheduling wise doesn't interfere with MCM operation past the school day, I wondered about.

578
00:52:38,470 --> 00:52:56,429
[SPEAKER_19]: going forward, that's going to remain the same based off of, I'm assuming, what's listed here, as well as are there limitations about having the shared space allowing for MCM to use a space more frequently because the media tech program, like the MHS film and television program production group,

579
00:52:58,370 --> 00:52:59,491
[SPEAKER_19]: Sorry, I'm not speaking properly.

580
00:52:59,951 --> 00:53:11,935
[SPEAKER_19]: So because the vocational program is using the space throughout the school day and then MCM uses it in the second portion of the day when school's over, are both programs being inhibited because of the shared space?

581
00:53:12,295 --> 00:53:20,937
[SPEAKER_19]: And so by removing the shared space and having the two separate ones, would that allow for both programs to use them or two entities to use them for longer portions of the day?

582
00:53:22,918 --> 00:53:27,954
[SPEAKER_19]: film and television, use it after school hours, and then MCM to use it during school hours, if that makes sense.

583
00:53:29,058 --> 00:53:30,959
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, no, I think it's a fair question.

584
00:53:31,300 --> 00:53:36,924
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's part of the conversation that we have been having with the team that has been working through this.

585
00:53:38,305 --> 00:53:49,913
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think the goal on everyone's part is to actually put a partnership agreement in place, which is really typical when you're sharing spaces like this, so that there are some rules of the road.

586
00:53:49,954 --> 00:53:55,558
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think right now, everything works pretty well because everybody communicates pretty effectively.

587
00:53:56,378 --> 00:54:08,547
[SPEAKER_04]: And we all know that, like, you know, as people change, as time goes on, assumptions get made and people are, you know, things become less clear or have the potential to become a little bit more murky.

588
00:54:09,127 --> 00:54:20,035
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the idea is that as we go forward, the MPS and Medford Community Media will build a formal partnership agreement that outlines

589
00:54:21,175 --> 00:54:34,523
[SPEAKER_04]: some of those sort of overlap times, if you will, and in times of overlap or conflict, how do we broker those times and spaces?

590
00:54:35,063 --> 00:54:44,288
[SPEAKER_04]: So more to come on that, and that actually won't necessarily wait until the new building, because that is a real need now, and it's commonly held.

591
00:54:44,909 --> 00:54:48,331
[SPEAKER_04]: I think the other thing that we'll explore as we talk about that, as Kevin alluded to,

592
00:54:49,437 --> 00:55:00,603
[SPEAKER_04]: Is there funding that we can leverage from the MCM capital funds to support some of the infrastructure costs, et cetera?

593
00:55:01,844 --> 00:55:15,751
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you know, you have like sort of the extension of those comment of those conversations, which is around like utilities and and the paying for it, like not rental, but like some sort of like, you know, compensation there or not.

594
00:55:16,331 --> 00:55:17,512
[SPEAKER_04]: But just instead of like,

595
00:55:17,946 --> 00:55:22,028
[SPEAKER_04]: the way it's always been done, to really thoughtfully work together to come up with those things.

596
00:55:22,108 --> 00:55:27,171
[SPEAKER_04]: So there are some overlap times, particularly if you think about extracurriculars.

597
00:55:27,931 --> 00:55:39,617
[SPEAKER_04]: And everyone's pretty committed to working through that and making sure it works for everybody, especially as the world of film and television changes pretty dramatically as we go forward.

598
00:55:39,657 --> 00:55:42,919
[SPEAKER_04]: So yeah, it's actually a really good question.

599
00:55:42,959 --> 00:55:46,341
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's one that the internal operations teams are going to continue to work on.

600
00:55:47,162 --> 00:55:47,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

601
00:55:47,522 --> 00:55:51,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and I can also pitch in a little bit on that, member Olopade.

602
00:55:52,983 --> 00:56:13,248
[SPEAKER_00]: To answer your question that you initially stated, I do think having the separate spaces would have provided a system where you could have the overlap and require a lot less effort, but

603
00:56:14,270 --> 00:56:24,634
[SPEAKER_00]: The way it stands right now, we could have some kind of overlap, again, as member Graham was referring to, with some collaboration.

604
00:56:24,674 --> 00:56:42,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Like one of the things that's come up between me and the film and TV teachers before is if we had our ducks in a row, depending upon the school, depending upon their schedule, we could have a member come in earlier than usual, let's say noon,

605
00:56:44,020 --> 00:56:50,222
[SPEAKER_00]: and have their studio production staffed by film and TV kids.

606
00:56:50,362 --> 00:56:55,484
[SPEAKER_00]: And because all members have been quarried anyway, you know, they're all OK to be on site.

607
00:56:56,324 --> 00:56:59,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could basically have a member get in earlier than usual.

608
00:56:59,985 --> 00:57:03,907
[SPEAKER_00]: You could still have the students getting some practical experience.

609
00:57:04,907 --> 00:57:11,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Um, and so like things like that, to give a more concrete example are things that we could do this fall.

610
00:57:11,510 --> 00:57:14,411
[SPEAKER_00]: If we are able to kind of juggle the various balls in the air.

611
00:57:15,071 --> 00:57:23,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Um, there's also elements where with this plan and, you know, I'm, I'm, I'm thinking of this plan a little bit more conceptually.

612
00:57:23,114 --> 00:57:28,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not really getting hung up on the actual diagram, but if we still have editing space.

613
00:57:29,657 --> 00:57:38,201
[SPEAKER_00]: we could theoretically tell members, you could we open at noon for editing and three o'clock for production, stuff like that.

614
00:57:38,281 --> 00:57:49,806
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, so there's there's definitely going to be ways that we will try, because I will say the way we've been doing it now is we're pretty dogmatic and strict about it just because it does make it a lot easier.

615
00:57:50,246 --> 00:57:53,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And we've been able to to flex with with good communication.

616
00:57:54,248 --> 00:57:57,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But a lot of members do wish there was some slightly more

617
00:57:58,530 --> 00:58:01,772
[SPEAKER_00]: uh, flexible earlier hours, but that varies from member to member.

618
00:58:02,393 --> 00:58:04,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh, everyone's schedule is different, but I don't want to go on too long.

619
00:58:05,034 --> 00:58:09,037
[SPEAKER_00]: I just want to give a couple specific examples that might help kind of flush it out.

620
00:58:10,058 --> 00:58:10,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

621
00:58:10,398 --> 00:58:10,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

622
00:58:10,678 --> 00:58:11,459
[SPEAKER_00]: That's really helpful insight.

623
00:58:11,479 --> 00:58:12,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Um, I appreciate it.

624
00:58:14,481 --> 00:58:14,881
[SPEAKER_04]: Thank you.

625
00:58:14,901 --> 00:58:18,784
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, I see that we have one hand from a member of the public, Paul Garrity.

626
00:58:18,804 --> 00:58:24,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Hi, Paul.

627
00:58:24,427 --> 00:58:25,348
[SPEAKER_04]: Name and address for the record.

628
00:58:26,260 --> 00:58:28,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Paul Geraghty, 40 Cedar Road in Medford.

629
00:58:28,281 --> 00:58:31,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Good evening, Madam Chair, members of the building committee.

630
00:58:31,061 --> 00:58:36,443
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to read a short piece here that I've sent to you all by email.

631
00:58:37,283 --> 00:58:39,643
[SPEAKER_01]: My name is Paul Geraghty.

632
00:58:39,663 --> 00:58:44,605
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm speaking tonight as a member of the Medford community engaged with the Medford community media's programming.

633
00:58:45,845 --> 00:58:50,586
[SPEAKER_01]: MCM and the vocational high school currently share production and cable cast infrastructure.

634
00:58:51,146 --> 00:58:54,767
[SPEAKER_01]: In addition, MCM provides public access and government programming.

635
00:58:55,333 --> 00:59:02,616
[SPEAKER_01]: As you finalize the space plan for the new high school, MCM should not be evaluated as an additional facility to lay it on top of a high school build.

636
00:59:03,316 --> 00:59:08,239
[SPEAKER_01]: And assigning a designation risks a miscalculation in both design and budget.

637
00:59:08,619 --> 00:59:11,580
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's three reasons why I wanna share with you that support this.

638
00:59:11,640 --> 00:59:14,561
[SPEAKER_01]: First, infrastructure.

639
00:59:14,721 --> 00:59:18,883
[SPEAKER_01]: MCM Studio isn't a standalone amenity that happens to reside inside the MHS complex.

640
00:59:21,525 --> 00:59:28,309
[SPEAKER_01]: It shares the same space, equipment, and technical staff used by the vocational school CTE program for the media and broadcasting.

641
00:59:29,050 --> 00:59:35,934
[SPEAKER_01]: If you vote to reduce or eliminate the space for MCM, you run the risk of impacting the CTE media and broadcasting curriculum.

642
00:59:36,775 --> 00:59:37,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Second, funding.

643
00:59:38,656 --> 00:59:47,662
[SPEAKER_01]: The City Cable franchise contains contractual revenue streams, a portion of which is allocated to the public access education and government.

644
00:59:48,839 --> 00:59:51,481
[SPEAKER_01]: in providing support and capital expenses.

645
00:59:52,422 --> 01:00:03,270
[SPEAKER_01]: The contract has provided 10 years worth of funding for capital expenses, and it also has provided PEG funding for ongoing public access, education, and government programming operations.

646
01:00:03,911 --> 01:00:05,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Third, access, and this is important.

647
01:00:06,933 --> 01:00:12,598
[SPEAKER_01]: The City Cable franchise is the authority for the existence of the public access, education, and government channels.

648
01:00:13,299 --> 01:00:17,342
[SPEAKER_01]: The franchise agreement requires the city to establish rules and regulations

649
01:00:18,091 --> 01:00:27,396
[SPEAKER_01]: for the use of the public access channel in facilities, which, as I said, currently reside at the high school space.

650
01:00:28,396 --> 01:00:33,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Public access use should not conflict with the educational use of the same facilities.

651
01:00:34,699 --> 01:00:40,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Public access requires access to the facilities outside the normal school day.

652
01:00:41,183 --> 01:00:46,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That sharing requirement can be solved with a separate external entrance and scheduling.

653
01:00:46,812 --> 01:00:49,593
[SPEAKER_01]: These requirements need to be addressed in the final bill.

654
01:00:50,233 --> 01:00:56,114
[SPEAKER_01]: To be clear, I support the MSBA shared space model rather than a standalone facility.

655
01:00:56,274 --> 01:00:59,395
[SPEAKER_01]: However, my concern has a narrow focus.

656
01:01:00,316 --> 01:01:10,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Please treat MCM space equipment and staffing as integral to the vocational media program requirements and not a separate line item to trim independently.

657
01:01:11,418 --> 01:01:13,219
[SPEAKER_01]: A properly sized shared space

658
01:01:14,196 --> 01:01:15,277
[SPEAKER_01]: is the right solution.

659
01:01:15,497 --> 01:01:21,241
[SPEAKER_01]: It's cost effective and can accommodate education, public access, and government programming.

660
01:01:21,742 --> 01:01:22,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

661
01:01:25,445 --> 01:01:25,925
[SPEAKER_04]: Thank you, Paul.

662
01:01:27,638 --> 01:01:28,318
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, okay.

663
01:01:28,578 --> 01:01:38,983
[SPEAKER_04]: So there is a recommendation here, um, for the team to consider, um, to eliminate the space, the 1400 square feet as noted on this slide.

664
01:01:39,003 --> 01:01:51,688
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, doing so tonight means this space will be formally and, you know, forever more removed from our, um, from consideration as we move into schematic design.

665
01:01:51,708 --> 01:01:54,049
[SPEAKER_04]: Um, is there any motion?

666
01:01:54,855 --> 01:01:57,456
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I think we have one more member of the public.

667
01:01:57,476 --> 01:01:58,576
[SPEAKER_04]: Hold on one second, folks.

668
01:01:59,417 --> 01:02:03,078
[SPEAKER_04]: Rob Carney.

669
01:02:03,098 --> 01:02:03,138
[SPEAKER_06]: Hi.

670
01:02:03,999 --> 01:02:04,439
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you.

671
01:02:05,659 --> 01:02:13,022
[SPEAKER_06]: So the high level comment that I want to make as a citizen here is, first of all, I thought Paul raised some great points.

672
01:02:13,082 --> 01:02:22,606
[SPEAKER_06]: But it's hard for me as a citizen when I'm thinking of the total price tag of this high school, which is already quite high, very high, actually.

673
01:02:23,106 --> 01:02:33,399
[SPEAKER_06]: To weigh in on a particular project or a cut without having an understanding of what the total dollar amount is of all potential cuts on the table.

674
01:02:34,020 --> 01:02:40,969
[SPEAKER_06]: I kind of think of this like, you know, with a home renovation right now, I have a fixer-upper right now, and we need to renovate every single room.

675
01:02:42,271 --> 01:02:53,599
[SPEAKER_06]: making decisions about whether to spend $5,000 on this or $2,000 on this, it's easier to make that decision when you understand what are the total costs are going to be.

676
01:02:53,699 --> 01:02:59,624
[SPEAKER_06]: So I understand that you guys want to go through this like kind of piece by piece.

677
01:02:59,804 --> 01:03:07,570
[SPEAKER_06]: But as a citizen here, I think it would be very helpful to see a total dashboard in some way, even if it means slowing down the process.

678
01:03:08,130 --> 01:03:25,118
[SPEAKER_06]: Of all the cuts that are on the table, especially given the just general anxiety that a lot of citizens are feeling about it, debt exclusion on the table, tax increases, et cetera, I think that would drive more public engagement and help people make more informed decisions, including your committee.

679
01:03:25,298 --> 01:03:25,598
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you.

680
01:03:26,947 --> 01:03:27,607
[SPEAKER_04]: Thanks, Rob.

681
01:03:28,648 --> 01:03:36,011
[SPEAKER_04]: Just so that you know, the point of this section of review is to look at space efficiency, right?

682
01:03:36,071 --> 01:03:41,094
[SPEAKER_04]: So we are looking at how do we just make sure that this building is going to be used maximally.

683
01:03:42,354 --> 01:03:51,825
[SPEAKER_04]: When we get to October and we are making those final decisions, I think that's where, to your point, you'll see total cost.

684
01:03:52,846 --> 01:03:59,473
[SPEAKER_04]: By then, we'll have more information about things like Curtis Tufts and whether that is a viable alternative for some components.

685
01:03:59,753 --> 01:04:06,039
[SPEAKER_04]: So there's many, many things that have to happen before we can really have that conversation from a financial perspective in earnest.

686
01:04:06,180 --> 01:04:11,485
[SPEAKER_04]: So the focus between now and the end of September is going to be on space efficiency.

687
01:04:12,146 --> 01:04:21,755
[SPEAKER_04]: And if that means that we can eliminate some space without altering the ed program and the ed plan, that is what we're here to do right now.

688
01:04:21,855 --> 01:04:21,995
[SPEAKER_04]: So

689
01:04:22,886 --> 01:04:31,910
[SPEAKER_04]: Your comment and your concern is well heard by this committee, and we will get there, but that's not where we are just yet.

690
01:04:32,010 --> 01:04:47,538
[SPEAKER_04]: So I'm just going to thank you for your patience and make sure that we are, in fact, crawling through every single space that we have so that we can really feel good about the fact that we have a maximum efficiency building.

691
01:04:48,398 --> 01:04:51,500
[SPEAKER_06]: If I may just offer a brief response to that.

692
01:04:53,041 --> 01:05:04,547
[SPEAKER_06]: The only issue here is, right, if we could see the total cost and potential cost cuts, let's say all the costs on the table could get us down to, I don't know, 600 million or something like that.

693
01:05:04,887 --> 01:05:14,292
[SPEAKER_06]: That may change the view maybe among some members of the committee here about, you know, how do we feel about Paul's comments about Medford Community Media, right?

694
01:05:14,352 --> 01:05:16,573
[SPEAKER_06]: If there were a lot of other cuts on the table that

695
01:05:17,262 --> 01:05:24,948
[SPEAKER_06]: could be higher, lower hanging fruit that might not have as much impact to organizations such as Medford Community Media.

696
01:05:25,468 --> 01:05:33,954
[SPEAKER_06]: I think having this high level financial overview first, I think needs to come first before you're talking about cutting square footage in space.

697
01:05:33,994 --> 01:05:35,636
[SPEAKER_06]: So that's just my view on this.

698
01:05:35,696 --> 01:05:37,177
[SPEAKER_06]: So thanks for your response.

699
01:05:37,937 --> 01:05:38,238
[SPEAKER_04]: Thanks.

700
01:05:39,278 --> 01:05:39,959
[SPEAKER_04]: Mayor Lungo-Kern.

701
01:05:40,732 --> 01:05:41,632
[SPEAKER_16]: Thank you, Member Graham.

702
01:05:42,653 --> 01:05:51,695
[SPEAKER_16]: I'm gonna move approval, but before the roll is called, if we could have somebody from SMMA or left field explain what the cost savings is here.

703
01:05:51,735 --> 01:06:02,459
[SPEAKER_16]: I think Rob raises a good point and that should at least be maybe at the bottom of each slide or mentioned during, I think we have like four or five of these tonight.

704
01:06:02,519 --> 01:06:06,660
[SPEAKER_16]: So just mention what the cost savings is if we do approve this vote.

705
01:06:14,437 --> 01:06:15,960
[SPEAKER_04]: Matt Rice, do you want to answer that?

706
01:06:16,641 --> 01:06:16,962
[SPEAKER_05]: Sure.

707
01:06:17,503 --> 01:06:23,594
[SPEAKER_05]: So we do have cost information at the end of the set of slides as we go through.

708
01:06:23,614 --> 01:06:25,939
[SPEAKER_05]: I do not know that we've

709
01:06:26,652 --> 01:06:32,676
[SPEAKER_05]: broken it out per each one of these square footage groupings that we have here.

710
01:06:33,397 --> 01:06:35,718
[SPEAKER_05]: But we do have a sum total at the end.

711
01:06:36,339 --> 01:06:46,006
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not sure if it would be helpful to jump down to that now to sort of see what that sum total would be and then circle back up, maybe not do it after every single one.

712
01:06:46,026 --> 01:06:54,752
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that's potentially the only thing I can offer that we have on hand to share right now as a cost piece if that were helpful.

713
01:06:56,437 --> 01:06:58,738
[SPEAKER_04]: What is our price per square foot?

714
01:07:00,658 --> 01:07:06,339
[SPEAKER_05]: It's a little bit over $1,000 a square foot, total project cost.

715
01:07:07,320 --> 01:07:15,761
[SPEAKER_04]: So I think just for the purposes of considering each of these incrementally, we're talking about 1,400 square feet.

716
01:07:15,781 --> 01:07:18,082
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you call it $1,000 a square foot, it's $1.4 million.

