1053
01:29:50,565 --> 01:29:55,109
[SPEAKER_04]: Matt, do you want to take us through this section and the change here?

1054
01:29:56,969 --> 01:29:57,269
[SPEAKER_05]: Sure.

1055
01:29:57,289 --> 01:30:03,713
[SPEAKER_05]: Um, and I think the change here is a very, um, straightforward one, um, but also impactful.

1056
01:30:04,054 --> 01:30:21,305
[SPEAKER_05]: Um, and I know that we had a lot of discussion initially about the number of seats, um, that were in the auditorium, um, as part of the PSR submission and the cost estimate, um, we were accounting for a thousand seats, um, within the auditorium, um, just from a spatial standpoint, as well as a cost standpoint.

1057
01:30:23,066 --> 01:30:33,256
[SPEAKER_05]: The proposal recommendation is to reduce from 1,000 seats to 7,500 seats because it is 10 square feet per seat.

1058
01:30:34,357 --> 01:30:38,521
[SPEAKER_05]: That's a direct reduction on the net square footage to usable side of 2,500 square feet.

1059
01:30:40,682 --> 01:30:44,083
[SPEAKER_05]: dropping us down to 7,500 square feet total for the auditorium.

1060
01:30:45,644 --> 01:30:54,226
[SPEAKER_05]: I can offer a little bit of additional commentary here about the dialogue that was had and how we arrived at that 750 seat number.

1061
01:30:55,166 --> 01:31:08,530
[SPEAKER_05]: It was very important for the school administration to have an environment where two classes, two entire classes could be brought together for an assembly and that was the main driver of the number because we're at just about 1,400 students

1062
01:31:10,223 --> 01:31:12,846
[SPEAKER_05]: for our design enrollment that was approved by MSBA.

1063
01:31:13,366 --> 01:31:25,116
[SPEAKER_05]: If we divide that in half, it's 700 students, and so that extra capacity of, say, 50 seats will allow for some staff to join those assemblies, but that meets the educational goal of what was there.

1064
01:31:25,817 --> 01:31:34,785
[SPEAKER_05]: We also, though, did talk about that number, the seating quantity, with members of the community, even some members of the building committee,

1065
01:31:36,145 --> 01:31:50,097
[SPEAKER_05]: about sort of the potential of the auditorium to function for outside rentals as well as what it felt like for theatrical, musical, and performances and concerts.

1066
01:31:50,977 --> 01:31:57,840
[SPEAKER_05]: And that 750 number also allows for a slightly more intimate environment, which was seen as a positive.

1067
01:31:58,801 --> 01:32:06,404
[SPEAKER_05]: And there was a request to realize that 750 seats incorporating a balcony into the auditorium space.

1068
01:32:07,285 --> 01:32:10,286
[SPEAKER_05]: So that is generally what's reflected here in terms of the plan.

1069
01:32:10,326 --> 01:32:11,907
[SPEAKER_05]: What it allows us to do is make the

1070
01:32:12,955 --> 01:32:24,197
[SPEAKER_05]: the long dimension of the auditorium space a little bit more compact, which will allow us to sort of fit that space into the overall building footprint a little bit more effectively.

1071
01:32:24,958 --> 01:32:30,839
[SPEAKER_05]: So I think there were a couple of positives that came out of those very detailed discussions on the auditorium.

1072
01:32:31,259 --> 01:32:41,961
[SPEAKER_05]: But again, this represents a large amount of savings that can be realized when we multiply that 2,500 net square feet by the 1.5 net to gross multiplier.

1073
01:32:46,182 --> 01:32:47,362
[SPEAKER_16]: Through the chair, may I ask a question?

1074
01:32:47,382 --> 01:32:48,923
[SPEAKER_16]: Sure.

1075
01:32:50,063 --> 01:32:55,985
[SPEAKER_16]: Matt, if we vote on this tonight to do the reduction, is that the last reduction we can do?

1076
01:32:56,665 --> 01:33:04,888
[SPEAKER_16]: Not that I want to reduce any further, but I do have an amendment out there just in case cost wise, we felt we needed to find other places to reduce come

1077
01:33:05,415 --> 01:33:11,882
[SPEAKER_16]: end of October where we're trying to get it down to a certain point, certain amount per tax taxpayer, tax households.

1078
01:33:12,603 --> 01:33:18,350
[SPEAKER_16]: So does this limit us to reduce further if we have to, although I don't want to, but.

1079
01:33:19,803 --> 01:33:21,204
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, I appreciate the question, Mayor.

1080
01:33:21,765 --> 01:33:25,788
[SPEAKER_05]: So I don't think there's anything that would preclude a further reduction after this.

1081
01:33:25,848 --> 01:33:28,931
[SPEAKER_05]: I think the committee is really sort of setting the process here.

1082
01:33:29,752 --> 01:33:33,635
[SPEAKER_05]: And I don't think there's been any discussion that would prevent that type of thing moving forward.

1083
01:33:34,116 --> 01:33:41,082
[SPEAKER_05]: I think the only thing that we need to keep our eye on is the October 6th date, which is the time when we have

1084
01:33:41,957 --> 01:33:50,722
[SPEAKER_05]: committed both to the building committee as well as the MSBA that we would be agreeing on what will be in the building and not in the building.

1085
01:33:50,782 --> 01:33:58,667
[SPEAKER_05]: So we want to try to arrive at any further reduction by October 6th if that's the direction that we choose to proceed in.

1086
01:33:58,687 --> 01:34:01,469
[SPEAKER_14]: Thank you.

1087
01:34:01,789 --> 01:34:01,969
[SPEAKER_14]: Luke?

1088
01:34:04,331 --> 01:34:09,401
[SPEAKER_14]: Matt, I wanted to ask about MSBA guidelines for a few of these categories.

1089
01:34:10,583 --> 01:34:16,694
[SPEAKER_14]: With the stage, is 2500 the guideline or does that exceed the guideline by 900 feet?

1090
01:34:19,778 --> 01:34:20,559
[SPEAKER_05]: Good question, Luke.

1091
01:34:20,719 --> 01:34:23,400
[SPEAKER_05]: And we do have a clarification as we get a little bit further down.

1092
01:34:23,420 --> 01:34:25,701
[SPEAKER_05]: We look at the totals that point out exactly that.

1093
01:34:26,521 --> 01:34:27,701
[SPEAKER_05]: OK.

1094
01:34:27,821 --> 01:34:37,866
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, the auditorium at 7,500 seats and 7,500 square feet does maximize the MSBA reimbursement for that program component, the larger piece.

1095
01:34:38,806 --> 01:34:41,767
[SPEAKER_05]: The stage for the MSBA guidelines is 1,600 square feet.

1096
01:34:41,787 --> 01:34:43,788
[SPEAKER_05]: So the 2,500 net square feet does exceed that by 900.

1097
01:34:48,870 --> 01:35:07,935
[SPEAKER_05]: The rationale behind why we're at 2500 square feet is because it matches the size of the existing stage and of the existing auditorium environment, the auditorium itself, the house where the seats are is definitely has many challenges from an educational and sort of performance aspect, but the stage.

1098
01:35:08,755 --> 01:35:13,860
[SPEAKER_05]: is seen as a real strength in terms of what it offers in terms of capacity and functionality.

1099
01:35:14,780 --> 01:35:25,249
[SPEAKER_05]: And we do see most MSBA projects exceed the stage size because 1,600 square feet is challenging to have a full set of

1100
01:35:26,610 --> 01:35:40,919
[SPEAKER_05]: rigging and sort of movement activities in space also for performers, especially when there are multiple events during the course of the year that bring in both high school performers, middle school performers, elementary school.

1101
01:35:42,040 --> 01:35:46,663
[SPEAKER_05]: The auditorium really does function as that central district-wide resource.

1102
01:35:47,628 --> 01:35:47,848
[SPEAKER_14]: Okay.

1103
01:35:48,389 --> 01:35:51,554
[SPEAKER_14]: And Matt, one more about MSBA reimbursement guidelines.

1104
01:35:52,235 --> 01:35:58,864
[SPEAKER_14]: The scene shop, I am under the impression that is non-reimbursable.

1105
01:35:58,924 --> 01:35:59,545
[SPEAKER_14]: Can you confirm?

1106
01:36:02,718 --> 01:36:21,664
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll have to go back Luke and look at this one, what I will point out, and you see the comment on the right hand side that the scene shop has been located under the technology and engineering rooms, which is where there is a little bit more variability in terms of what's reimbursable I do believe that we are under the overall

1107
01:36:22,488 --> 01:36:25,349
[SPEAKER_05]: guideline number in that category right now.

1108
01:36:26,410 --> 01:36:40,235
[SPEAKER_05]: So that may have been deemed acceptable as reimbursable, but I do need to go back and check the comments that went back to the MSBA as part of the PSR report and our responses to them.

1109
01:36:40,575 --> 01:36:44,117
[SPEAKER_05]: Those will have some indication as to what is reimbursable.

1110
01:36:44,177 --> 01:36:46,818
[SPEAKER_05]: I know we're going to touch on that a little bit later in the presentation.

1111
01:36:47,673 --> 01:36:53,258
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, no, I'd appreciate that, because I think we currently have a scene shop, and it's 740 square feet.

1112
01:36:54,499 --> 01:36:56,902
[SPEAKER_14]: So there's precedent for having a scene shop.

1113
01:36:58,023 --> 01:37:05,149
[SPEAKER_14]: But I feel like there's more savings to be had with the stage.

1114
01:37:06,230 --> 01:37:09,574
[SPEAKER_14]: Being 900 feet over reimbursable is expensive.

1115
01:37:10,595 --> 01:37:10,815
[SPEAKER_14]: Thank you.

1116
01:37:25,645 --> 01:37:25,925
[SPEAKER_22]: Paul?

1117
01:37:28,527 --> 01:37:31,629
[SPEAKER_07]: Yeah, so Luke, I just want to say thanks for the question about the stage.

1118
01:37:31,669 --> 01:37:34,351
[SPEAKER_07]: It is a really good question, because it's really large.

1119
01:37:35,251 --> 01:37:42,416
[SPEAKER_07]: And right, a portion of that is non-reimbursable, even if the space comes down to the 750 magic number.

1120
01:37:42,976 --> 01:37:52,483
[SPEAKER_07]: I can share with you a little bit of the discussions that are going on with the theater consultant, which is, first of all, really, although it's in the high school,

1121
01:37:53,525 --> 01:37:58,757
[SPEAKER_07]: there are multiple events that happen where

1122
01:37:59,955 --> 01:38:06,097
[SPEAKER_07]: Elementary, middle, and high school orchestras and musicians are all sharing the space for concerts.

1123
01:38:06,237 --> 01:38:14,080
[SPEAKER_07]: In the past, they've been all combined at the Chevalier Theater, which has introduced a number of different challenges for using an outside space.

1124
01:38:14,780 --> 01:38:21,242
[SPEAKER_07]: And so it offers support for existing educational programs and music and the arts that are already happening.

1125
01:38:22,082 --> 01:38:27,964
[SPEAKER_07]: And to reduce that space could potentially make it more difficult than what we currently have.

1126
01:38:29,995 --> 01:38:53,246
[SPEAKER_07]: Another component that is related to the scene shop, which is yes, the scene shop is potentially one of those things that while it can directly support the auditorium, it could be worked into reimbursable space for the CIT programs, and it could have some additional usefulness for those programs that we could get that space reimbursed.

1127
01:38:54,467 --> 01:38:58,429
[SPEAKER_07]: If we can't, and that's another option on the chopping block for later,

1128
01:38:59,321 --> 01:39:13,450
[SPEAKER_07]: Another reason why keeping square footage on the stage itself is a good idea at this point is that one of the key things that we would want for a new auditorium is acoustical treatments that are flexible.

1129
01:39:14,410 --> 01:39:26,998
[SPEAKER_07]: since orchestral performance is one of the main uses of that space, via improving the acoustics while still having a very large and useful stage area, I think would be ideal.

1130
01:39:27,258 --> 01:39:42,767
[SPEAKER_07]: And it would be a real missed opportunity if we can get a new building and we don't have a way to have a built-in acoustical shell that can be removed when we need the full space for events or drama or musical theater, but can be installed for acoustic

1131
01:39:44,303 --> 01:39:44,863
[SPEAKER_07]: performance.

1132
01:39:45,904 --> 01:39:54,348
[SPEAKER_07]: And those acoustical shells, they do fold up and they can get pretty small compared to how big they look when you see on stage.

1133
01:39:54,428 --> 01:40:01,772
[SPEAKER_07]: There's big clouds in the sky and walls on the side and a backdrop, but they don't get shorter.

1134
01:40:01,912 --> 01:40:04,054
[SPEAKER_07]: They pack tighter, but they don't get shorter.

1135
01:40:04,574 --> 01:40:10,557
[SPEAKER_07]: And so if the scene shop ends up being somehow shoehorned into something and its ceiling is only 11 feet tall,

1136
01:40:12,598 --> 01:40:25,287
[SPEAKER_07]: then we're going to be stuck with, we cut this acoustical shell, or every single event that uses the stage has this enormous white object that's shoved upstage left and really compromises the use of the space.

1137
01:40:25,487 --> 01:40:33,773
[SPEAKER_07]: So by keeping the square footage on the stage, that's our only guarantee that we're going to have full height two-story

1138
01:40:34,794 --> 01:40:52,540
[SPEAKER_07]: usable space for this necessary stage equipment that will make the space really valuable, not just for the musicians and the students who play there, but also for any potential outside renters that would want to use that or community partners that would want to use it for musical performance.

1139
01:40:54,240 --> 01:41:02,623
[SPEAKER_07]: So I hope that helps understand why I think keeping it at 2,500 square feet is particularly important at this point.

1140
01:41:03,429 --> 01:41:28,118
[SPEAKER_07]: One thing for the team, just to call out, I know that the floor plans are still in flux, but with the community multipurpose going away completely, just wanted to call out that I don't see in this area right now, aside from the chorus and band rooms, any spaces that have square footage comparable to the size of the stage.

1141
01:41:29,008 --> 01:41:49,800
[SPEAKER_07]: So I think in further discussions about that, it would be useful to try and find a way that we're funding multiple purposes for this valuable space, but there's a way that a dance performance or a musical performance that really needs to replicate the usable stage area for taping out, for laying out, for doing full choreography or whatever.

1142
01:41:51,676 --> 01:42:01,979
[SPEAKER_07]: would have some other room in the facility so that if the auditorium is occupied with other programming, renters, other events, that there is a place to rehearse.

1143
01:42:02,139 --> 01:42:08,981
[SPEAKER_07]: Otherwise, it limits all rehearsal of the real program to the stage itself, which

1144
01:42:09,617 --> 01:42:18,880
[SPEAKER_07]: tends to cause some pretty big scheduling problems because that stage becomes very valuable for gathering and assemblies and other things.

1145
01:42:18,941 --> 01:42:26,043
[SPEAKER_07]: So I think that there's still definitely enough square footage total in the building to find that space.

1146
01:42:26,683 --> 01:42:30,665
[SPEAKER_07]: It just would be a shame if it's like, oh, well, this thing was shaped like this, so it's long and skinny.

1147
01:42:31,025 --> 01:42:32,466
[SPEAKER_07]: And this one is long and skinny the other way.

1148
01:42:32,506 --> 01:42:38,948
[SPEAKER_07]: And it turns out there is actually no way you could tape out the actual stage performance area anywhere in the building except the stage itself.

1149
01:42:41,920 --> 01:42:42,460
[SPEAKER_14]: Thank you, Paul.

1150
01:42:44,122 --> 01:42:44,582
[SPEAKER_14]: Hey, Matt.

1151
01:42:45,122 --> 01:42:46,163
[SPEAKER_14]: Yeah, follow up on the stage.

1152
01:42:46,223 --> 01:42:48,485
[SPEAKER_14]: So grossing factor, right?

1153
01:42:48,825 --> 01:42:49,645
[SPEAKER_14]: 50%, that's free 50%.

1154
01:42:49,866 --> 01:42:52,207
[SPEAKER_14]: The stage is not like a room.

1155
01:42:52,688 --> 01:42:53,968
[SPEAKER_14]: It doesn't have four walls.

1156
01:42:55,289 --> 01:43:06,257
[SPEAKER_14]: Is there flexibility in applying the grossing factor associated with 1,600 square feet to build out a nearly 2,500 square foot stage?

1157
01:43:07,018 --> 01:43:07,998
[SPEAKER_14]: Has that been explored?

1158
01:43:08,759 --> 01:43:10,160
[SPEAKER_14]: Is that in the art of the possible?

1159
01:43:11,349 --> 01:43:14,645
[SPEAKER_14]: And then with regards to that scene shop.

1160
01:43:15,361 --> 01:43:20,823
[SPEAKER_14]: Um, I'm pretty sure it's non-reimbursable based on the space summary that was, was flowed to us.

1161
01:43:21,663 --> 01:43:25,165
[SPEAKER_14]: Um, but I'm basing my, my judgment solely on that.

1162
01:43:25,285 --> 01:43:33,788
[SPEAKER_14]: So, um, anyway, for what it's worth, but back to the, uh, grossing factor, is there a way to utilize grossing factor?

1163
01:43:33,908 --> 01:43:36,549
[SPEAKER_14]: Because 2,500 square feet times 1.5 is really big, but 1600 times 1.5 is actually pretty close to 2,500.

1164
01:43:36,729 --> 01:43:37,449
[SPEAKER_14]: Is there some,

1165
01:43:43,311 --> 01:43:46,352
[SPEAKER_14]: flexibility, some margin, because we don't have four walls.

1166
01:43:49,173 --> 01:43:51,213
[SPEAKER_05]: All right, so let me take those two questions.

1167
01:43:51,253 --> 01:44:05,317
[SPEAKER_05]: I know there was a hand that went up from the superintendent as well, but the question on whether or not we can selectively apply grossing factors on a per program space, it's interesting, Luke.

1168
01:44:05,377 --> 01:44:08,058
[SPEAKER_05]: It's something that crosses my mind all the time.

1169
01:44:08,798 --> 01:44:10,939
[SPEAKER_05]: At the same time, after having done a lot of these,

1170
01:44:12,060 --> 01:44:19,087
[SPEAKER_05]: There is some amount of equalization across the entirety of the plan, the building.

1171
01:44:20,128 --> 01:44:29,378
[SPEAKER_05]: When we look at that grossing factor and how it's applied in this particular instance, yes, we don't have sort of full walls around it, but we do have a fairly long hallway.

1172
01:44:30,170 --> 01:44:35,415
[SPEAKER_05]: that is located immediately adjacent to the stage and as a result of the stage being an opaque object at that time.

1173
01:44:36,295 --> 01:44:41,520
[SPEAKER_05]: So a lot of that square footage is likely to be contributing to that hallway space.

1174
01:44:41,600 --> 01:44:53,150
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, the chorus room will cover half of it for its grossing factor, but we don't ever get to the point of trying to break that down in terms of the grossing factor.

1175
01:44:54,170 --> 01:44:55,531
[SPEAKER_05]: for the addition construction.

1176
01:44:55,611 --> 01:44:59,594
[SPEAKER_05]: For the renovated space, it's a little bit of a different calculus, right?

1177
01:44:59,654 --> 01:45:03,637
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the renovated space is the renovated space and there's space around it.

1178
01:45:04,637 --> 01:45:06,338
[SPEAKER_05]: So we treat it slightly different there.

1179
01:45:07,119 --> 01:45:18,066
[SPEAKER_05]: But again, we don't ever break out and sort of apply different grossing factors based on the localized area of the plan and the program that we're talking about.

1180
01:45:19,937 --> 01:45:24,578
[SPEAKER_05]: And I will just add also that Helen is actually doing some good research in the background.

1181
01:45:24,618 --> 01:45:26,319
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why it's helpful that we have a big team.

1182
01:45:26,339 --> 01:45:35,321
[SPEAKER_05]: And we actually do believe that the scenes shop is reimbursable under the technology and engineering rooms category from our perspective.

1183
01:45:35,381 --> 01:45:36,642
[SPEAKER_04]: I was just going to say that, Matt.

1184
01:45:36,782 --> 01:45:41,463
[SPEAKER_04]: I just pulled up the documents from MSBA in our response.

1185
01:45:42,243 --> 01:45:45,424
[SPEAKER_04]: And that section says the district is proposing a total of 9,280 square feet.

1186
01:45:49,488 --> 01:45:53,872
[SPEAKER_04]: which is below the MSBA guidelines by 7,120 square feet.

1187
01:45:54,232 --> 01:45:58,235
[SPEAKER_04]: The proposed area in this category is decreased by 4,000 since PDP.

1188
01:45:59,016 --> 01:46:00,617
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it lists the four programs.

1189
01:46:00,837 --> 01:46:09,464
[SPEAKER_04]: And they had a question about the Innovation Lab, which we responded to elsewhere, where they were not going to share cost share.

1190
01:46:09,484 --> 01:46:13,027
[SPEAKER_04]: They clearly said that they don't object to the space.

1191
01:46:13,067 --> 01:46:16,570
[SPEAKER_04]: However, it's considered ineligible, and that was not present in this section.

1192
01:46:21,059 --> 01:46:27,229
[SPEAKER_04]: Potentially more to come, but they were very clear where they were not willing to cost share elsewhere in the document.

1193
01:46:30,732 --> 01:46:32,974
[SPEAKER_25]: Well, that took care of one of the reasons I raised my hand.

1194
01:46:34,556 --> 01:46:46,548
[SPEAKER_25]: But the second thing is I just wanted to, I know that there is an advanced slide that talks a little bit more, less on this table and floor plan.

1195
01:46:46,868 --> 01:46:47,569
[SPEAKER_25]: Yes, thank you.

1196
01:46:47,609 --> 01:46:50,812
[SPEAKER_25]: I think this might be important too for the people that are tuning in.

1197
01:46:51,853 --> 01:46:58,376
[SPEAKER_25]: as well as for me just to say how much I appreciate attending all of these meetings because of how much I learn.

1198
01:46:58,636 --> 01:47:07,020
[SPEAKER_25]: I've learned so much from Paul Malone and from the theater expert that I really appreciate the conversations.

1199
01:47:08,420 --> 01:47:17,064
[SPEAKER_25]: I feel like it's a it's another language sometimes I can't fully follow along when we're talking about the rigging and things and things like that but

1200
01:47:17,764 --> 01:47:26,466
[SPEAKER_25]: Part of the conversation has also been the experience for our performers, as well as the experience for the audience.

1201
01:47:27,566 --> 01:47:33,567
[SPEAKER_25]: And anyone that's been in the little theater knows that it's not great for either, but especially the audience.

1202
01:47:34,267 --> 01:47:46,030
[SPEAKER_25]: And part of that was also the distance between where the audience sits and is positioned, and then how far back the stage goes, as well as the part that has to be accessible.

1203
01:47:47,531 --> 01:47:55,456
[SPEAKER_25]: And so a lot of the conversations had to do with that experience and having the audience feel a little closer to the performance.

1204
01:47:56,336 --> 01:48:05,142
[SPEAKER_25]: And I know Paul mentioned it, but I just have to say, because it is also like a point of pride, but Medford has such a thriving arts community.

1205
01:48:05,342 --> 01:48:19,118
[SPEAKER_25]: program from elementary all the way up to high school and it's really a community that values very much the fine and performing arts and so our orchestra and our band programs are

1206
01:48:20,313 --> 01:48:26,399
[SPEAKER_25]: quite, there's a lot of participation, they're quite successful, and they keep growing each year.

1207
01:48:26,739 --> 01:48:40,913
[SPEAKER_25]: So I know Paul mentioned it, but when there are orchestra concerts, there are about 100 students on the stage, and that is something that happens not just once a year, but a couple times throughout the year where concerts are happening.

1208
01:48:41,914 --> 01:48:52,158
[SPEAKER_25]: And informational sessions are happening too, where students are coming in to see what it is all about in terms of orchestra and band.

1209
01:48:52,198 --> 01:48:57,900
[SPEAKER_25]: And so it's very important for us to be able to still capitalize on that space.

1210
01:48:59,819 --> 01:49:06,684
[SPEAKER_25]: Paul mentioned, but Chevalier is really difficult for our families to get to, to park, the accessibility of that.

1211
01:49:06,724 --> 01:49:17,733
[SPEAKER_25]: And for us to like relegate performances there where all of our students from elementary to middle school to high school can fit on a stage, that's not necessarily as accessible for our families.

1212
01:49:17,853 --> 01:49:26,000
[SPEAKER_25]: And maintaining a stage where the whole community can come together for our orchestra concerts is to me a non-negotiable.

1213
01:49:26,360 --> 01:49:28,842
[SPEAKER_25]: And I just wanted to make sure that that part was

1214
01:49:30,386 --> 01:49:31,408
[SPEAKER_25]: really double down on.

1215
01:49:34,314 --> 01:49:34,695
[SPEAKER_04]: Thank you.

